    - Tool descriptions, the pointer stub aide_init writes into the
      host's config file, and the methodology doc hub aide_init
      installs onto the host's disk are all composed from the canonical
      doc files under docs/ at build or init time. The server's source
      code contains no AIDE doctrine as string literals, so a doc edit
      is the only way to change what this submodule ships.
    - Behavior is identical across every supported MCP client (Claude
      Code, Cursor, Windsurf, Copilot, Zed). Client-specific logic is
      confined to aide_init; the core four tools are client-agnostic.
    - aide_init turns a cold host project AIDE-ready in one command and
      is safely re-runnable. Pointer stub written to the host's config
      file, methodology doc hub installed onto the host's disk, slash
      commands scaffolded for every pipeline phase, MCP server wired
      into the project's MCP config, IDE file associations configured.
      The stub names the doc-hub path the installer writes into, so the
      two sides cannot drift. Every step is idempotent.
  undesired:
    - A tool description that points at an external document or says the
      agent should "read CLAUDE.md for details." The handshake is the
      only documentation surface this channel is guaranteed to deliver.
    - A tool description, pointer-stub teaching sentence, or
      methodology doc hand-written as a string literal in this
      submodule's source, duplicating content that lives in docs/. The
      duplicate drifts on the first edit to either side and breaks the
      single-source-of-truth invariant inherited from the parent.
    - A pointer stub that names a host-side doc-hub path different
      from the path the installer actually writes into. The two sides
      must derive from a single shared source inside this submodule;
      any arrangement that admits drift between them is broken on
      arrival even if it passes its unit tests.
    - Divergent behavior between MCP clients on the same input. If Claude
      Code and Cursor see different results from `aide_discover`, the
      server has failed its client-parity invariant.
    - aide_init that is not idempotent — a project initialized twice
      with stacked pointer stubs, duplicated doc-hub files, duplicated
      slash commands, or a broken .mcp.json is a corrupted project,
      not an initialized one.
    - A tool added to the surface because a responsibility "didn't quite
      fit" an existing tool, when the responsibility could be absorbed
      by one of the existing tools without overlap. Redundant tool surface
      fragments the handshake and forces agents to guess which tool to
      call.
    - Truncated descriptions justified by "token budget." Tokens spent
      teaching are recovered every time an agent doesn't have to fetch
      an external doc or silently violate the convention.
---

## Context

This submodule implements both delivery channels the parent spec requires.
The handshake channel matters because it is the only documentation surface
a compliant MCP client is required to present to the model verbatim, in
every session, in every project, without user action. The aide_init channel
matters because a one-command bootstrap is the only onboarding path that
does not depend on the installing agent remembering or paraphrasing what
AIDE is. Between the two, the parent's "no paraphrasing, no per-project
drift" guarantee lives or dies here.

Everything else — how many tools, how descriptions are written, how init
handles idempotency — is in service of keeping those two channels faithful
to the canonical docs above.

## Strategy

**The tool list is the primary teaching artifact for channel one, not an
API reference.** Each description introduces the concept it invokes before
invoking it: `aide_discover` explains what `.aide` files are and why you
read them before code; `aide_scaffold` explains the naming-conflict rule
before it describes its `type` enum. An agent that only ever reads the
tool list must still come away with the full convention. The cost of a
longer description is recovered every time an agent doesn't need to fetch
an external doc, and dwarfed by the cost of an agent silently violating
the convention.

**Tool descriptions and host-installed methodology artifacts are composed
from docs/, never hand-written.** The canonical doc files under docs/ are
read at build time (for tool description text) and at init time (for both
the pointer stub aide_init writes into the host's config file and the
doc-hub files aide_init lands on the host's disk). No AIDE doctrine lives
as a string literal in this submodule's source — doctrine lives in docs/,
and this submodule is a renderer. This is how the parent's
single-source-of-truth invariant survives contact with the server's
runtime.

**Delivery to the host practices progressive disclosure at the file
layer, not just at the doc layer.** aide_init does not dump the full
methodology into the host's config file. It writes a short pointer stub
into the config file and lands the full methodology doc set into a
dedicated doc hub on the host's disk. The config file — which the agent
reads on every session — carries only the knowledge that AIDE exists,
where the hub lives, and when to crawl into it. The hub itself is read
only by sessions whose current task actually touches `.aide` files. The
pattern mirrors how `study-playbook` works against the user's global
brain: a shallow pointer surface that enumerates a deep content surface,
with the agent crawling on demand instead of carrying everything on
every read.

**Each tool covers a distinct lifecycle phase with no overlap.** Discover
is the map; read is the drill; scaffold is the pen; validate is the health
check; init is the bootstrap. Adding a new tool requires showing that no
existing tool can absorb the responsibility without overlap. A tighter
surface means less for an agent to get wrong and less description budget
competing for attention at handshake time.

**Client parity via MCP, not per-client shims.** The server speaks
standard MCP over stdio. All client-specific branching lives in
`aide_init`, which writes config files for whichever framework it
detects. The core four tools are fed identical input schemas regardless
of which client is talking, because parity is what lets the teaching
surface be reused across clients.

**Idempotency as an invariant, not a feature.** Init, scaffold, and
validate are all safe to re-run on a project already in that state.
Agents retry tool calls when uncertain; a non-idempotent call corrupts
on the retry. This is especially load-bearing for init, which writes
files an agent should never feel nervous about re-running.

## Good examples

A new agent connects to aidemd-mcp for the first time and, before
touching a single file, reasons:

> There are .aide spec files next to orchestrator code that I should
> read before I read the code. Five types exist: `.aide` is the default
> intent spec; `intent.aide` is the same thing renamed when a
> `research.aide` sits alongside; `research.aide` holds raw research;
> `plan.aide` is the architect's checkboxed implementation plan;
> `todo.aide` is a QA re-alignment document. I must never see both `.aide` and
> `intent.aide` in the same folder. My workflow is: call `aide_discover`
> with no path for the project map, then drill into the relevant subtree
> with a path, then `aide_read` specific specs as needed.

Every piece of that reasoning came from the tool list alone. No
CLAUDE.md was read; no README was fetched. That is the target experience
at handshake time.

A user runs `aide_init` on a fresh project and gets back a summary like:

> Detected framework: Claude Code.
> Created CLAUDE.md with AIDE pointer stub naming the doc hub at .aide/.
> Installed .aide/ doc hub with seven canonical methodology docs and a hub index.
> Created slash commands for research, spec, synthesize, plan, build, QA, and fix phases.
> Wired aidemd-mcp into .mcp.json.
> Configured .zed/settings.json to render .aide as Markdown.
> Skipped VS Code extension — `code` CLI not on PATH.

Running the same command a second time returns the same summary with
"already present" markers instead of "created", and nothing on disk
changes.

A maintainer edits `docs/aide-spec.md` to clarify the placement rule.
They rebuild the server; the next agent to connect sees the updated text
in the `aide_discover` tool description because the description template
reads from the doc at build time. They also run `aide_init` on a host
project; the updated text lands in the host's `.aide/aide-spec.md`
because the doc-hub installer reads the same canonical source. One edit
propagated through both channels — handshake teaching and installed
host-side content — with no duplication in between.

An agent in a host project opens a file unrelated to AIDE to fix a bug.
It reads CLAUDE.md at session start, sees the short pointer stub, notes
that the `.aide/` doc hub exists, and consciously skips crawling it
because the current task touches no `.aide` files. Total AIDE cost paid
by that session: a handful of lines of stub. Later the same agent picks
up a task that does touch `.aide` files, follows the stub's pointer into
`.aide/`, reads the hub index, and drills into `aide-spec.md` and
`aide-template.md` — and only those. The delivery layer practiced the
same progressive disclosure the methodology teaches.

## Bad examples

A tool description that reads "Scaffold new .aide files. See the AIDE
docs for naming rules." The agent has learned nothing about the
naming-conflict rule and will happily create a `.aide` next to an
existing `research.aide`, producing exactly the corruption the tool was
built to prevent. The description failed its only job.

A tool description hand-written as a template literal in
`src/tools/scaffold/index.ts`, containing AIDE rules the maintainer
typed from memory. The canonical doc under docs/ has since been updated;
the literal has not; the two now disagree, and the server's handshake
ships a different doctrine than `aide_init` does. The
single-source-of-truth invariant has been broken at the submodule level.

An `aide_init` that appends its pointer stub to CLAUDE.md on every
run, so a project initialized three times has three stacked
marker-bounded regions. Idempotency was treated as a nice-to-have
instead of an invariant, and the file is now corrupted.

An `aide_init` that skips the doc-hub step and dumps the full
methodology body inside CLAUDE.md's stub region instead. Every non-
AIDE session in the host now re-reads several hundred lines of
doctrine on every open, and the refactor that this shape was meant
to enforce has been quietly reversed at the delivery layer while
passing every test.

A tool — say `aide_lint` — added to the surface because validation rules
"felt different" from health checks. Both tools show up in the handshake,
overlap in responsibility, and the agent has to guess which one to call.
The surface has been diluted for no gain, because the responsibility should
have lived inside validate.
